Title:
BestEdrOfTheMarket V3 — Kernel-Mode EDR Driver (BEOTM) using callbacks, VAD analysis, and Alternative System Call Handlers

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
This write-up describes the third iteration of “BestEdrOfTheMarket” (BEOTM), a Windows EDR-like project that moved from user-mode injection/hooking to a kernel-mode driver architecture. The driver is designed to be largely autonomous, collecting telemetry via documented kernel notification callbacks and an undocumented-but-supported alternative system call handler mechanism, then sending captured buffers/events to a minimal user-mode client for YARA scanning and UI. It focuses on detecting/mitigating common offensive TTPs such as process hollowing/ghosting (via VAD tree vs PEB/LDR inconsistencies), code injection (thread creation + stack/VAD checks), direct syscalls, suspicious syscall parameter buffers (e.g., NtWriteVirtualMemory/NtWriteFile), registry persistence, and LSASS access for credential dumping. The post includes practical kernel-internals details (structures, offsets, trap frame argument recovery, handler registration/unload pitfalls) and implementation references into the open-source repo. It’s useful for EDR/defensive engineering, Windows kernel researchers, and red teamers who want to understand what kernel telemetry can realistically catch and how. Interesting because it demonstrates syscall interception without SSDT patching and leverages VAD-based integrity checks that mirror forensic techniques.

Technical Focus:
- Windows kernel driver telemetry and architecture (IOCTLs, queues, callbacks)
- VAD tree traversal and LDR/PEB integrity comparisons for hollowing detection
- Alternative System Call Handlers (PsRegisterAltSystemCallHandler / PsAltSystemCallHandlers) and trap-frame based syscall argument capture
- Syscall-parameter buffer capture + user-mode YARA scanning pipeline
- Thread creation monitoring for injection detection; stack/VAD validation
- ObRegisterCallbacks-based LSASS handle access prevention; registry callbacks for persistence

Use Cases:
- Build a research/educational kernel-mode EDR prototype for Windows 10 (20H1–22H2)
- Detect process hollowing/ghosting/herpaderping-like anomalies using VAD vs LDR contradictions
- Intercept and inspect sensitive syscall buffers (memory writes, file writes, memory protection changes)
- Block/alert on LSASS memory read handle opens indicative of credential dumping
- Study and operationalize alternative syscall handler interception as an SSDT-avoiding technique
- Generate kernel telemetry for lab detection engineering and adversary simulation validation

Keywords:
Windows kernel driver, EDR, PsSetCreateProcessNotifyRoutineEx, PsSetCreateThreadNotifyRoutine, PsSetLoadImageNotifyRoutine, CmRegisterCallbackEx, ObRegisterCallbacks, VAD tree, _MMVAD, _RTL_AVL_TREE, PEB, LDR, process hollowing, ghosting, herpaderping, alternative system call handler, PsAltSystemCallHandlers, PsRegisterAltSystemCallHandler, _KTRAP_FRAME, direct syscalls, NtWriteVirtualMemory, NtProtectVirtualMemory, NtWriteFile, LSASS, PROCESS_VM_READ, YARA, IOCTL, CET, shadow stack, IA32_PL3_SSP